  C String Programs

>  Topic: Character arrays & strings, implemented without <string.h>
> Programs: Concatenate · Compare · Search · Delete

Golden rule: a C string is a 'char' array ending with '\0'. Every loop below stops when it sees '\0' (which is '0', i.e. false).


 1. Concatenation two strings (add / merge)

Logic: find the end of d, then copy 's' from there. Finish with '\0'.

#include <stdio.h>
int main(void)
{
    char s[10], d[20];
    int ld, i, j;

    printf("Enter src and dst string: ");
    scanf("%9s %9s", s, d);
    printf("Before: %s\n", d);

    for (ld = 0; d[ld]; ld++);            // ld = length of d 

    for (i = 0, j = ld; s[i]; i++, j++)
        d[j] = s[i];
    d[j] = '\0';

    printf("After: %s\n", d);
    return 0;
}


Memory view ( s = "ab",  d = "123",  ld = 3)

| Step  | Operation      | d after step   |

| 1     |  d[3] = s[0]   | 1 2 3 a        |
| 2     |  d[4] = s[1]   | 1 2 3 a b      |
| 3     |  d[5] =  \0    | 1 2 3 a b \0   |


Output

Enter src and dst string: ab 123
Before: 123
After: 123ab




 2. Compare two strings (compare all characters)

Why not if (s1 == s2) ? It is - invalid for this purpose: it compares only the base addresses of the arrays, not their contents.

#include <stdio.h>
int main(void)
{
    char s1[10], s2[10];
    int i;

    printf("Enter two strings:\n");
    scanf("%9s %9s", s1, s2);

    for (i = 0; s1[i] && s2[i]; i++)
        if (s1[i] != s2[i])
            break;

    if (s1[i] == s2[i])
        printf("equal\n");
    else
        printf("different\n");
    return 0;
}


| Input 1 | Input 2 | Output    | Reason                        |

|  abcd   |  abcd   | equal     | both reach '\0' together      |
|  Abcd   | abcd    | different | A != 'a' (case-sensitive)     |
|  abcd   | abcdef  | different |  s1 ends, s2 still has 'e'    |


 3. Search a char in a string

Logic: scan left to right; on the first match print and - return. If the loop ends, the char is absent.


#include <stdio.h>
int main(void)
{
    char s[20], ch;
    int i;

    printf("Enter a string:\n");
    scanf("%19s", s);
    printf("Enter a char:\n");
    scanf(" %c", &ch);                    // space skips leftover newline 

    for (i = 0; s[i]; i++) {
        if (s[i] == ch) {
            printf("char is present\n");
            return 0;
        }
    }
    printf("char is not present\n");
    return 0;
}


Output

Enter a string: embedded
Enter a char: d
char is present


 4. Delete a char from a string

Logic: when s[i] == ch, shift everything after it one position left (s[j] = s[j+1]). Then do i-- so the same index is checked again (the next char may also match).


#include <stdio.h>
int main(void)
{
    char s[20], ch;
    int i, j;

    printf("Enter a string:\n");
    scanf("%19s", s);
    printf("Enter a char:\n");
    scanf(" %c", &ch);
    printf("Before: %s\n", s);

    for (i = 0; s[i]; i++) {
        if (s[i] == ch) {
            for (j = i; s[j]; j++)
                s[j] = s[j + 1];
            i--;                          // check same index again 
        }
    }
    printf("After: %s\n", s);
    return 0;
}


Dry run: "embedded", delete 'e'

| Pass           | String            |
| Start          |  e m b e d d e d  |
| Remove 1st   e |  m b e d d e d    |
| Remove 2nd   e |  m b d d e d      |
| Remove 3rd  e  |  m b d d d        |


Output

Enter string: embedded
Enter char: e
Before: embedded
After: mbddd



 Key Takeaways
 
- Strings end with '\0'; loops use it as the stopping condition.
- Never compare strings with ==; compare character by character.
- Use %9s / %19s in scanf to avoid buffer overflow.
- Delete in place = shift left + i--.
- These same skills apply to UART buffers and command parsing in embedded firmware.
